  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\u222c}_{R}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${\u222c}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${\u222c}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099625/assets/5c5dbc673c2c6a9bd0a4f06223133e21e2dcbbdbaa2dfe2de2b29d8824dd821e.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements II and III are true. In both cases, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
